<p>A puzzle from the <a href="https://onedrive.live.com/?redeem=aHR0cHM6Ly8xZHJ2Lm1zL2YvcyFBcHZHbTRNZ1FCT19nUkp1QjJRSnFSdzlIMGxyP2U9dHloU1hF&#038;cid=BF134020839BC69B&#038;sb=name&#038;sd=1&#038;id=BF134020839BC69B%21177&#038;parId=BF134020839BC69B%21147&#038;o=OneUp">1975-1976 issue</a> of <a href="https://onedrive.live.com/?id=BF134020839BC69B%21146&#038;cid=BF134020839BC69B&#038;redeem=aHR0cHM6Ly8xZHJ2Lm1zL2YvcyFBcHZHbTRNZ1FCT19nUkp1QjJRSnFSdzlIMGxyP2U9dHloU1hF"><em>Eureka</em></a>, the journal of the Cambridge University Mathematical Society:</p>
<p>A jet fighter on the surface of the Earth is being chased by two identical fighters, all with ample fuel. Can the pursuers get within shooting range, no matter how they start out?</p>

